1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
It started with a simple question.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:09,800
Something that should have taken an afternoon.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:11,000
Instead it took three weeks.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,400
I kept a notepad beside my keyboard, and every assumption I had written down turned out to be wrong.

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:23,600
Now the pipeline runs on a server in a cold aisle, blinking quietly in the dark.

6
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:28,000
The work is finished, and finished work is easy to measure.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,400
A city street after dark, rain on the pavement, nobody waiting for anything in particular.

8
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:37,000
That is the part I did not expect.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,200
Someone in the market noticed the machine was slow, and asked why it was slow.

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:47,600
I had an answer.

11
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:53,000
Most mornings begin the same way, with the window open and the kettle on.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,400
But this one had a train platform in it, and a departure board, and a long grey morning waiting.

13
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:03,000
The lesson was not about speed.

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,500
It was about noticing that the slow part was never the part you thought it was, and that a hand plane on oak teaches you more about that than any diagram.

15
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:15,000
So I stopped trying to make it faster, and went back to the bench.